sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant O as Lock holder
    participant W as Usage worker
    participant D as PostgreSQL
    Note over O,D: Two pending rows: customer A first, customer B second.<br/>customer_billing_state.customer_id = B is unlocked.
    O->>D: BEGIN, FOR UPDATE<br/>customer_billing_state.customer_id = A
    W->>D: SELECT pending usage_inbox key<br/>ORDER BY received_at, source, event_id LIMIT 1
    D-->>W: Oldest event belongs to customer A
    W->>D: BEGIN, request FOR UPDATE<br/>customer_billing_state.customer_id = A
    Note over W,D: WAIT for A, even though customer B could run
    D-->>W: Attempt deadline expires, rollback
    Note over W,D: Neither row is processed or quarantined.<br/>A later attempt selects A again while it remains oldest.<br/>More identical worker loops do not remove this bottleneck.
    O->>D: COMMIT, release customer A
    W->>D: Next attempt locks A, shared catalog, event row<br/>Account A's usage_inbox row and COMMIT
    W->>D: Next attempt locks B, shared catalog, event row<br/>Account B's usage_inbox row and COMMIT
    Note over W,D: Adjacent valid case: lock only later customer B.<br/>The oldest unlocked customer A can still complete.<br/>Proposed scheduling improvement must lock account before event row.
